---

<Base
  sky={0.35}
  title="Anchorfile · VelaWind"
  type="article"
  description="A command-line tool that reads a repository and writes the context file an AI agent needs."
>
  <main class="page" id="main">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/">VelaWind</a> · <a href="/projects">Projects</a></p>

    <h1>Anchorfile</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A command-line tool that reads a repository and writes the context file an
      AI agent needs.
      <br />
      <a href={npmUrl}>npm package</a> · <a href={repoUrl}>Source</a>
    </p>

    <CaseShot href="/projects/anchorfile" />

    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      An AI coding agent starts every session knowing nothing about the
      repository it is in. Claude Code reads a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> file at
      the root for that: the stack, the scripts, the layout, the conventions.
      Written by hand, that file is accurate on the day it is written and then
      goes stale, because nothing connects it to the files it describes. A
      dependency changes, a script is renamed, and the agent goes on acting on
      the old description.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the obvious approach was not used</h2>
    <p>
      The obvious tool for writing a description of a codebase now is a
      language model: point it at the repository and ask for the file. That
      replaces one unchecked text with another. A model's summary can be wrong
      in ways that read as right, and a context file is the one document every
      later session trusts without looking. A wrong line there is repeated
      session after session.
    </p>
    <p>
      So Anchorfile reads only what is on disk: <code>package.json</code>,
      lockfiles, config files, <code>requirements.txt</code> and the top-level
      tree. Every line it writes comes from a file a reader can open, and the
      same repository always produces the same output. The judgement calls a
      file tree cannot answer, what to avoid and which files matter most, are
      left as marked sections for a person to fill in.
    </p>

    <h2>One trade-off</h2>
    <p>
      Detection is deliberately shallow. Frameworks are matched by exact
      package name, language by counting extensions in one directory, and an
      ESLint flat config, the default since ESLint 9, is not recognised. The
      cost is that Anchorfile misses things a model would have guessed. The
      reason to accept it is that a miss is visible: the file says "None
      detected" and a person can correct it, while a confident wrong guess looks
      exactly like a right one.
    </p>

    <h2>What should change</h2>
    <p>
      The package was on npm before it had a single test. The suite came later:
      fixture repositories for each kind of project the README says it reads,
      the two documented limits pinned so they change only on purpose, a
      snapshot of the emitted file, and the built CLI run end to end. Writing it
      found one defect straight away: top-level folders were listed in whatever
      order the filesystem returned them, so the same repository could produce
      two different files on two machines. For a tool whose whole claim is that
      its output follows from the files, that is the wrong defect to have
      shipped.
    </p>
    <p>
      The limits above remain. Python dependencies are read
      from <code>requirements.txt</code> only, not <code>pyproject.toml</code>,
      and flat ESLint config is still unrecognised. Both are tested as limits,
      not fixed.
    </p>

    <p class="provenance">
      The rest of this page is fetched from
      the <a href={sourceUrl}>Anchorfile README</a> at build time and rendered
      here, so it is maintained in the repository and not in this site. It
      covers the architecture, the tests and, under "Known limits", what does
      not work.
    </p>

    <div class="readme" set:html={html} />
  </main>
</Base>
